# 02-expand-contract run 2026-09-26T15:53:19Z
== fresh Postgres (docker compose down -v && up) ==
 Container 02-expand-contract-postgres-1 Healthy 
== demo: rename users.name -> display_name while old and new app versions run side by side ==

## 0. Starting point
   concept: schema has users.name, v1 runs in production
   v1 (write name, read name): write ok, read 1 rows, all have a name

## Naive: rename in one step
   concept: RENAME breaks every running instance the moment it commits; a rolling deploy always has old code running
   migration naive: ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN name TO display_name
   v1 (write name, read name): FAILS: column "name" of relation "users" does not exist
   migration undo: ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN display_name TO name

## 1. Expand
   concept: only additive, backward-compatible changes: add the new column, relax NOT NULL on the old one; v1 and v2 run side by side
   migration M1: ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN display_name TEXT, ALTER COLUMN name DROP NOT NULL
   v1 (write name, read name): write ok, read 2 rows, all have a name
   v2 (write both, read name): write ok, read 3 rows, all have a name
   too early: reading the new column before the backfill would show blanks
   probe v3 (write both, read display_name): 2 rows WITHOUT a name

## 2. Migrate data
   concept: v1 is fully retired, so every writer now fills both columns; backfill the old rows
   migration M2: UPDATE users SET display_name = name WHERE display_name IS NULL
   v2 (write both, read name): write ok, read 4 rows, all have a name

## 3. Switch reads
   concept: deploy v3 which reads the new column; v2 and v3 overlap during the rollout
   v2 (write both, read name): write ok, read 5 rows, all have a name
   v3 (write both, read display_name): write ok, read 6 rows, all have a name

## 4. Stop writing the old column
   concept: all writers fill display_name and the backfill is done, so it can become NOT NULL; deploy v4, overlapping with v3
   migration M3: ALTER TABLE users ALTER COLUMN display_name SET NOT NULL
   v3 (write both, read display_name): write ok, read 7 rows, all have a name
   v4 (write display_name, read display_name): write ok, read 8 rows, all have a name

## 5. Contract
   concept: v3 is fully retired, nobody reads or writes name any more: drop it
   migration M4: ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN name
   v4 (write display_name, read display_name): write ok, read 9 rows, all have a name

## Why the order matters
   concept: had v3 still been running, the contract step would have broken it
   v3 (write both, read display_name): FAILS: column "name" of relation "users" does not exist

